{"proposal":{"arguments":[],"assumptions":[{"assumption_id":"node.7cda521da78c24227e5a7440.a0","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Paragraph 10 is read principally as allocating continuing accountability; separate substantive requirements retain independent force.","status":"PROVISIONAL"},{"assumption_id":"node.7cda521da78c24227e5a7440.a1","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Governance powers and supervisory evidence are possible evidence of retained responsibility, not an enumerated safe harbour.","status":"PROVISIONAL"}],"bundle_hash":null,"controlled_language":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 principally preserve the Product Provider's ultimate accountability despite outsourced performance. Authority to obtain information, supervise and secure correction could support retention; actual supervisory conduct could support discharge under an applicable standard. Neither establishes proper records or operational soundness by itself. The packet supplies no sufficient test for concluding that the requirement has been fulfilled.","family_ids":["scope"],"parent_id":null,"revision_reason":"A provider with effective intervention powers and active supervision may have retained responsibility even when a vendor produces defective ownership records. On this reading, that defect engages substantive record-keeping requirements without itself proving that accountability was transferred. Paper allocation alone does not establish effective retention or fulfillment.","source_packet_hash":"d74180e126dda06342060a17b37c3227fc695e9fc0f0d5ce68b187d7cb8bc612","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"],"subject_unit":"Paragraph 10 as continuing allocation of responsibility"},"reason":"Reserve space for unexplored families","run_id":"run.8adc12b432874c5bb1e8b4730a0ab93f"}